    {
      "title": "Knitting in the Round",
      "slug": "knitting-in-the-round",
      "displayOrder": 6,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 443,
        "lessonOrder": 6
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "title": "Three Videos with Text",
          "slug": "three-videos-1782474092000",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3918,
            "blockType": "HTML",
            "editorLayout": "threeVideosWithText"
          },
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<p>With a ribber and a standard gauge machine you can have up to 400 needles at your fingertips!\nDid you know that you can knit in the round? (no seams). BUT, remember automatic patterning (punchcard or electronics) only happens on the main bed. So knitting in the round is strictly for stockinette only</p>",
              "legacyComponentId": 6572,
              "order": 1,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosIntro"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "178356914",
              "title": "Circular (tubular) Knitting",
              "legacyComponentId": 6573,
              "order": 3,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosVideo1"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "",
              "legacyComponentId": 6574,
              "order": 2,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosCaption1"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859457",
              "title": "Matching Ribber and Main Bed Tension",
              "legacyComponentId": 6575,
              "order": 5,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosVideo2"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "",
              "legacyComponentId": 6576,
              "order": 4,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosCaption2"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859462",
              "title": "Half Circular / Tubular",
              "legacyComponentId": 6577,
              "order": 7,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosVideo3"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Another option is to create WIDE fabrics with both beds in work.  Instead of a tube, you can create a single piece of knitting that is up to 400 needles wide!  It takes a bit of concentration, and it will only produce stockinette.",
              "legacyComponentId": 6578,
              "order": 6,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosCaption3"
            },
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "",
              "legacyComponentId": 6579,
              "order": 8,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosOutro"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Full Needle Rib",
      "slug": "full-needle-rib",
      "displayOrder": 7,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 444,
        "lessonOrder": 7
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "title": "Full Needle Rib Cast On Techniques",
          "slug": "text-video-1782474934952",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3919,
            "blockType": "HTML",
            "editorLayout": "textVideo"
          },
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "By putting all needles in work and knitting them at the same time you will produce a dense fabric that has knit stitches showing on both sides. True<a data-GlossaryId='184' class='glossaryhelp'> full-needle rib <i class='fa fa-question-circle' aria-hidden='true'></i></a>\nis completely reversible.<br><br>\nYou will want to use a thinner yarn.<br><br>\nBy adding stitch patterning on the main bed (color and/or texture) you can produce beautiful fabrics.<br><br>\nAdding multi-color knitting on the main bed while working full needle rib is called\n<a data-GlossaryId='268' class='glossaryhelp'> Double Bed Jacquard <i class='fa fa-question-circle' aria-hidden='true'></i></a>\nDouble Bed Jacquard. This technique is outside the scope of this course, but you MUST know how to set up and knit in full needle rib before attempting Jacquard.\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 6581,
              "order": 1,
              "layoutRole": "textVideoLeft"
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859303",
              "title": null,
              "legacyComponentId": 6582,
              "order": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "The best bind off technique for full needle rib is a \"Kitchener\" bind off by hand. Yes it's tedious, but done correctly you won't be able to tell a tubular cast on from this tubular bind off method.<br>Don't be afraid of this technique, practice makes perfection!",
              "legacyComponentId": 5870,
              "order": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151858966",
              "title": "Double Bed Bind Off",
              "legacyComponentId": 5871,
              "order": 4
            }
          ],
          "title": "Full Needle Rib Bind Off",
          "slug": "fnr",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 3918,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Flat Ribs",
      "slug": "flat-ribs",
      "displayOrder": 8,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 446,
        "lessonOrder": 8
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "title": "You have to try this",
          "slug": "three-videos-1782475269432",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4322,
            "blockType": "HTML",
            "editorLayout": "threeVideosWithText"
          },
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Do you love the look of both knit and purl stitches? Do you love the look of ribbing, but don't need or want the stretch?<br>Maybe your yarn isn't suited for ribbing, but you still want the texture ....\n\n<b>Flat Ribs are the answer!</b><br>Using the full needle rib setup as the basis, flat ribs fabric and hems have unlimited possibilities!<br>This video introduces you to the technique. The follow up video takes you step by step to mastering it.\n<br>You won't look at \"plain 'ol\" ribbing the same way again!<br>",
              "legacyComponentId": 6583,
              "order": 1,
              "layoutRole": "threeVideosIntro"
            },
            {
